🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗶𝘀 𝗥𝘂𝗻𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴, 𝗕𝘂𝘁 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝘁𝗮𝗶𝗻𝗲𝗿 𝗶𝘀 𝗨𝗻𝗵𝗲𝗮𝗹𝘁𝗵𝘆 — 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗗𝗼?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “Your Kubernetes pod is in Running state, but the application is not receiving traffic. How will you troubleshoot it?”

A pod showing Running doesn't necessarily mean the application is ready to receive traffic.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

1️⃣ Check pod status

kubectl get pods -n <namespace>

Look at the READY column.

For example:

NAME        READY   STATUS
app-pod     0/1     Running

The container is running, but it isn't ready.

2️⃣ Check pod details

kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Check the Events section for errors.

3️⃣ Check readiness probe

I would verify whether the readinessProbe is failing.

Common causes:

🔹 Wrong endpoint
🔹 Wrong port
🔹 Application hasn't started completely
🔹 Dependency/database unavailable
🔹 Incorrect probe configuration

4️⃣ Check application logs

kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Look for startup errors, connection failures, exceptions, or dependency issues.

5️⃣ Check Service endpoints

kubectl get endpoints <service-name> -n <namespace>

If the pod isn't ready, it may not appear as a usable endpoint for the Service.

6️⃣ Test the application directly

If appropriate, test from inside the cluster or through port-forwarding:

kubectl port-forward pod/<pod-name> 8080:8080

Then:

curl http://localhost:8080/health

🎯 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄 𝗜 𝗙𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗼𝘄:

Pod Running → READY status → Readiness Probe → Logs → Service Endpoints → Application Health

𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗹𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

Running ≠ Ready ≠ Healthy

This is an important distinction in Kubernetes troubleshooting.

Before making changes, identify where the request path is breaking and then fix the actual root cause. 
